{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LPiNs58R86cvHd2DAGmE9H","type":"tool_result","content":"370\t#\n371\t# It does NOT rewrite the decision vocabulary here — the grok block->deny\n372\t# rewrite below (also grok-gated) is the ESSENTIAL fix, because grok\n373\t# fail-OPENs on `{\"decision\":\"block\"}` EVEN WITH exit 2 (probe P5). This\n374\t# exit map is the secondary half; the meta-test asserts BOTH.\n375\t#\n376\t# CEO_HOOK_EXIT_MAP=0 disables the mapping (escape hatch if a future grok\n377\t# release turns exit-2-hostile; the arming check surfaces the override).\n378\t\n379\t# The grok path has TWO halves with DIFFERENT disable semantics:\n380\t#   - vocabulary rewrite (block->deny): the ESSENTIAL grok fix — a `block`\n381\t#     fail-OPENs on grok (P4/P5). NOT disableable by CEO_HOOK_EXIT_MAP.\n382\t#   - exit-2 mapping (deny->exit 2): belt-and-suspenders. CEO_HOOK_EXIT_MAP=0\n383\t#     turns this off (a future grok release that makes exit-2 hostile).\n384\t# Both fire ONLY under CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER=grok on a BLOCKING event; every other\n385\t# path preserves the historical exec byte-for-byte (Claude/Codex unchanged).\n386\t_CEO_GROK_PATH=0\n387\tif [ \"${CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER:-}\" = \"grok\" ]; then\n388\t  _CEO_GROK_PATH=1\n389\tfi\n390\t\n391\t# Event source: under grok, GROK_HOOK_EVENT is the AUTHORITATIVE source (grok\n392\t# sets it). Pair-rail S269 finding #4: a generic\n393\t# `${CLAUDE_HOOK_EVENT:-${GROK_HOOK_EVENT:-…}}` precedence lets a stale/injected\n394\t# CLAUDE_HOOK_EVENT=post_tool_use mislabel a real grok pre_tool_use as passive\n395\t# and DISABLE the chokepoint (grok then fail-opens). Since this whole block is\n396\t# already gated on CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER=grok, read grok's own var first.\n397\t_CEO_EVENT=\"${GROK_HOOK_EVENT:-${CLAUDE_HOOK_EVENT:-${CODEX_HOOK_EVENT:-}}}\"\n398\t_CEO_BLOCKING_EVENT=1\n399\tcase \"$_CEO_EVENT\" in\n400\t  # Passive events (grok spells them snake_case). A passive hook can never\n401\t  # block on grok, so neither the rewrite nor the exit map should touch it.\n402\t  post_tool_use|post_tool_use_failure|session_start|session_end|\\\n403\t  notification|pre_compact|post_compact|subagent_start|subagent_stop|\\\n404\t  permission_denied|stop_failure|stop)\n405\t    _CEO_BLOCKING_EVENT=0\n406\t    ;;\n407\tesac\n408\t\n409\t# Registered hooks have invocation deadlines: exit must leave spool work pending.\n410\texport CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT=1\n411\t\n412\tif [ \"$_CEO_GROK_PATH\" -eq 0 ] || [ \"$_CEO_BLOCKING_EVENT\" -eq 0 ]; then\n413\t  # Not the grok blocking path (Claude/Codex byte-identical to the historical\n414\t  # shim), or a passive grok event: preserve the historical exec (zero added\n415\t  # latency; exit = the hook's own).\n416\t  exec \"$FOUND_PY\" \"$HOOK_SCRIPT\" \"$@\"\n417\tfi\n418\t\n419\t# Blocking event: capture stdout so the decision can drive the exit code.\n420\t# stderr passes through untouched (breadcrumbs stay visible to the host).\n421\t# `set -e` must not kill us on a nonzero hook exit — we inspect it.\n422\tset +e\n423\t_CEO_HOOK_STDOUT=\"$(\"$FOUND_PY\" \"$HOOK_SCRIPT\" \"$@\")\"\n424\t_CEO_HOOK_RC=$?\n425\tset -e\n426\t\n427\t# --- grok vocabulary backstop (probe P5) ------------------------------------\n428\t#\n429\t# Hooks that route their egress through `_lib/adapters/resolve()` already\n430\t# emit `deny` under CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER=grok (grok.py:write_decision). But a\n431\t# LEGACY hook that hardcodes the Claude vocabulary — today\n432\t# `check_codex_filewrite.py`, which json.dumps `{\"decision\": \"block\"}`\n433\t# directly (lines 275/294/356) — would emit a word grok does not know.\n434\t# On grok that is malformed output = hook failure = **fail-OPEN, even\n435\t# with exit 2** (P5). Re-routing that hook through this shim (PLAN-156\n436\t# Wave 2 settings edit) gives it the exit mapping but NOT the vocabulary,\n437\t# so the shim translates here as well: one chokepoint owns BOTH halves,\n438\t# and any future hook that forgets the adapter seam degrades to a correct\n439\t# deny instead of a silent allow.\n440\t#\n441\t# We are guaranteed here to be on the grok blocking path. Rewrite block→deny\n442\t# with a WHITESPACE-TOLERANT regex (pair-rail S269 finding #3): the two\n443\t# fixed-string substitutions only matched json.dumps's exact spacing\n444\t# (`\"decision\": \"block\"` and `\"decision\":\"block\"`), so a hook emitting\n445\t# `\"decision\" : \"block\"` (space before the colon) would slip through and\n446\t# grok would fail-OPEN. `[[:space:]]*` around the colon covers any spacing.\n447\t# sed is one cheap subprocess on the (rare) blocking path — acceptable vs a\n448\t# silent-allow on an unusual-but-valid deny shape.\n449\tif [ -n \"$_CEO_HOOK_STDOUT\" ]; then"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f14aefec-dd31-41ef-96b5-86b1c0aa1232","uuid":"94949052-6851-4440-a5bf-f9b0a0dbf265","timestamp":"2026-11-15T10:42:56.544Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.tG8bbW/tree/.claude/hooks/_python-hook.sh","content":"#\n# It does NOT rewrite the decision vocabulary here — the grok block->deny\n# rewrite below (also grok-gated) is the ESSENTIAL fix, because grok\n# fail-OPENs on `{\"decision\":\"block\"}` EVEN WITH exit 2 (probe P5). This\n# exit map is the secondary half; the meta-test asserts BOTH.\n#\n# CEO_HOOK_EXIT_MAP=0 disables the mapping (escape hatch if a future grok\n# release turns exit-2-hostile; the arming check surfaces the override).\n\n# The grok path has TWO halves with DIFFERENT disable semantics:\n#   - vocabulary rewrite (block->deny): the ESSENTIAL grok fix — a `block`\n#     fail-OPENs on grok (P4/P5). NOT disableable by CEO_HOOK_EXIT_MAP.\n#   - exit-2 mapping (deny->exit 2): belt-and-suspenders. CEO_HOOK_EXIT_MAP=0\n#     turns this off (a future grok release that makes exit-2 hostile).\n# Both fire ONLY under CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER=grok on a BLOCKING event; every other\n# path preserves the historical exec byte-for-byte (Claude/Codex unchanged).\n_CEO_GROK_PATH=0\nif [ \"${CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER:-}\" = \"grok\" ]; then\n  _CEO_GROK_PATH=1\nfi\n\n# Event source: under grok, GROK_HOOK_EVENT is the AUTHORITATIVE source (grok\n# sets it). Pair-rail S269 finding #4: a generic\n# `${CLAUDE_HOOK_EVENT:-${GROK_HOOK_EVENT:-…}}` precedence lets a stale/injected\n# CLAUDE_HOOK_EVENT=post_tool_use mislabel a real grok pre_tool_use as passive\n# and DISABLE the chokepoint (grok then fail-opens). Since this whole block is\n# already gated on CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER=grok, read grok's own var first.\n_CEO_EVENT=\"${GROK_HOOK_EVENT:-${CLAUDE_HOOK_EVENT:-${CODEX_HOOK_EVENT:-}}}\"\n_CEO_BLOCKING_EVENT=1\ncase \"$_CEO_EVENT\" in\n  # Passive events (grok spells them snake_case). A passive hook can never\n  # block on grok, so neither the rewrite nor the exit map should touch it.\n  post_tool_use|post_tool_use_failure|session_start|session_end|\\\n  notification|pre_compact|post_compact|subagent_start|subagent_stop|\\\n  permission_denied|stop_failure|stop)\n    _CEO_BLOCKING_EVENT=0\n    ;;\nesac\n\n# Registered hooks have invocation deadlines: exit must leave spool work pending.\nexport CEO_HOOK_BOUNDED_EXIT=1\n\nif [ \"$_CEO_GROK_PATH\" -eq 0 ] || [ \"$_CEO_BLOCKING_EVENT\" -eq 0 ]; then\n  # Not the grok blocking path (Claude/Codex byte-identical to the historical\n  # shim), or a passive grok event: preserve the historical exec (zero added\n  # latency; exit = the hook's own).\n  exec \"$FOUND_PY\" \"$HOOK_SCRIPT\" \"$@\"\nfi\n\n# Blocking event: capture stdout so the decision can drive the exit code.\n# stderr passes through untouched (breadcrumbs stay visible to the host).\n# `set -e` must not kill us on a nonzero hook exit — we inspect it.\nset +e\n_CEO_HOOK_STDOUT=\"$(\"$FOUND_PY\" \"$HOOK_SCRIPT\" \"$@\")\"\n_CEO_HOOK_RC=$?\nset -e\n\n# --- grok vocabulary backstop (probe P5) ------------------------------------\n#\n# Hooks that route their egress through `_lib/adapters/resolve()` already\n# emit `deny` under CEO_HOOK_ADAPTER=grok (grok.py:write_decision). But a\n# LEGACY hook that hardcodes the Claude vocabulary — today\n# `check_codex_filewrite.py`, which json.dumps `{\"decision\": \"block\"}`\n# directly (lines 275/294/356) — would emit a word grok does not know.\n# On grok that is malformed output = hook failure = **fail-OPEN, even\n# with exit 2** (P5). Re-routing that hook through this shim (PLAN-156\n# Wave 2 settings edit) gives it the exit mapping but NOT the vocabulary,\n# so the shim translates here as well: one chokepoint owns BOTH halves,\n# and any future hook that forgets the adapter seam degrades to a correct\n# deny instead of a silent allow.\n#\n# We are guaranteed here to be on the grok blocking path. Rewrite block→deny\n# with a WHITESPACE-TOLERANT regex (pair-rail S269 finding #3): the two\n# fixed-string substitutions only matched json.dumps's exact spacing\n# (`\"decision\": \"block\"` and `\"decision\":\"block\"`), so a hook emitting\n# `\"decision\" : \"block\"` (space before the colon) would slip through and\n# grok would fail-OPEN. `[[:space:]]*` around the colon covers any spacing.\n# sed is one cheap subprocess on the (rare) blocking path — acceptable vs a\n# silent-allow on an unusual-but-valid deny shape.\nif [ -n \"$_CEO_HOOK_STDOUT\" ]; then","numLines":80,"startLine":370,"totalLines":549}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LPiNs58R86cvHd2DAGmE9H","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
